{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 39, :comments ["<b>And you shall see it.</b> It is a commandment that it be seen, and from this it follows that the commandment of tzitzit does not apply at night; therefore women are exempt from it, for it is a positive commandment caused by time. <b>And you shall remember all the commandments of HaShem and do them.</b> Seeing brings one to remembrance, and remembrance brings one to action. <b>And you shall not go astray after your heart and after your eyes.</b> The heart and the eye are the agents of transgression: the eye sees and the heart desires and the body commits the transgression. And if one does so, it is as though he sees the Throne of Glory, which is what the tekhelet resembles." "<b>And behold,</b> the tzitzit is to be for a sign and a reminder, so that a person should not pursue the musings of the heart and all that his eyes seek. And the reason the heart was mentioned first is because the heart leads the entire body, and all the limbs of the body follow after it."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:47.223126030Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}